WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.600
Today we are going to use an AI&nbsp;
to take a collection of photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.760
and hopefully create a digital human out of them.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:22.160
However, not so fast! For instance, have a look&nbsp;
at NVIDIA’s amazing face generator AI. As you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.120
it can generate beautiful results,&nbsp;
and they even show us the illusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:34.400
of these characters moving around, however, this&nbsp;
does not have a strong concept of 3D information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.280
These are 2D images, and 3D consistency&nbsp;
is not that great. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:49.600
Well, look. This person just one frame away is&nbsp;
not exactly the same person. For instance, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:57.360
hair moves around. This means that these images&nbsp;
are not art directable, or at least, not easily.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.160
As you see here, it has improved&nbsp;
a great deal over two years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.800
one more research paper down the&nbsp;
line, it’s still not quite there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.720
So, fine details, checkmark, 3D&nbsp;
consistency, not quite there.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:20.240
So let’s have a look at solutions that have&nbsp;
more 3D consistency. To be able to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.840
we can take a collection of photos like&nbsp;
these, and magically, create a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.760
where we can fly through these photos. It is&nbsp;
really crazy, because this is possible today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:40.240
for instance, here is NVIDIAs method that can be&nbsp;
trained to perform this in a matter of seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.720
This is remarkable, especially that&nbsp;
the input is only a handful of photos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.160
Some information is given about the&nbsp;
scene, but this is really not much.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.720
So, can we have a solution with 3D consistency?&nbsp;
Yes, indeed, for these, 3D consistency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.240
checkmark. However, the amount of fine&nbsp;
details in these outputs is not that great.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.760
Do you see the pattern here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:17.280
Depending on which previous method we use, we&nbsp;
either get tons of detail, but no 3D consistency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.520
or we can get our highly coveted 3D&nbsp;
consistency, but then, the details are gone.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.000
So, is the dream dead? No digital humans for us?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:36.160
Well, don’t despair, and have a look at this new&nbsp;
technique that promises one megapixel images that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.600
are also consistent. Details and consistency&nbsp;
at the same time! When I first saw this paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.960
I said I will believe it when I see&nbsp;
it, so, let’s have a look together.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:54.320
This is StyleNERF, a previous technique, and we&nbsp;
see the problems that we now expect - the hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.640
is not consistent, the earring is flickering&nbsp;
a great deal, and there are other issues too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:07.680
So, are the authors of the new paper&nbsp;
claiming that they can solve all this? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.440
now, hold on to your papers, and have a&nbsp;
look at this. This is the new technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.520
Oh my goodness. The earring, facial features&nbsp;
and the hair stay still as we rotate them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.320
and it indeed shows the new&nbsp;
angles correctly. I love it!

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.280
Let’s examine this phenomenon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.560
in a little more detail. Look at the hair&nbsp;
here with StyleNERF. It looks decent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:44.000
but it’s not quite there. And I really wonder why&nbsp;
that is. Let’s zoom in and have a closer look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:52.160
Oh yes, upon closer inspection, this hair is a&nbsp;
bit of a mess. And, with the new technique, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:04:00.080
that is what I call consistency across the video&nbsp;
frames. Smooth hair strands everywhere. So good!

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.240
And what I absolutely loved about this new work&nbsp;
is that it outperforms this previous technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.080
which is from how many years ago? Well, if&nbsp;
you have been holding on to your papers, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.480
squeeze that paper, because this work is&nbsp;
from not even one year ago, just from 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:25.600
months ago. And you already see a meaningful&nbsp;
improvement over that. That is insanity.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.920
Now, not even this technique is perfect. I&nbsp;
don’t know for sure if the hair consistency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.160
is perfect here and we are dealing&nbsp;
with video compression artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.880
or whether this is still not a 100% there,&nbsp;
but this truly is a great step forward.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.600
Also, here, you see that the images are&nbsp;
still not as detailed as the photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:57.120
but this seems to be a roadblock to me that is&nbsp;
much easier to solve than 3D consistency. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.520
impression is that with this, the most difficult&nbsp;
part of the task is already done, and just one or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:10.000
two papers down the line, and I am sure we will&nbsp;
be seeing even more realistic virtual humans.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.400
So, can we enter into a virtual world as a&nbsp;
digital human from just a collection of photos?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:23.840
Oh yes! Time to meet our beloved ones from afar,&nbsp;
or meet new people, and play some games together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.960
What a time to be alive! So,&nbsp;
does this get your mind going?&nbsp;&nbsp;

